        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Do I need technical skills to write effective AI prompts?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "No. Prompt writing requires clear communication, not coding. Anyone who can write a task brief or a clear email can learn how to write AI prompt instructions that work. The skill is entirely language-based and accessible to beginners from any academic or professional background."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I reuse AI prompts across projects?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. Saving high-performing prompts as templates is standard practice in AI content workflow management. Swap out variables like topic, audience, and format while keeping the ROLE + TASK + AUDIENCE + FORMAT + CONSTRAINT structure fixed. Freelancers who build reusable prompt libraries report handling 2–3x more client work per week without increasing their working hours."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the ROLE + TASK + AUDIENCE + FORMAT + CONSTRAINT formula for AI prompts?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The ROLE + TASK + AUDIENCE + FORMAT + CONSTRAINT formula is the standard structure for writing effective AI prompts. Each element serves a specific purpose: ROLE tells the AI who it is and what expertise to apply; TASK states exactly what output is needed; AUDIENCE defines who the content is for including age, profession, or knowledge level; FORMAT specifies how the output should be structured such as bullets, paragraphs, or a table with word limits; CONSTRAINT defines what to avoid including specific words, phrases, or formatting choices. This formula applies to 90% of common use cases and is the foundation of any repeatable AI content workflow."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What are the most common mistakes when writing AI prompts?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The 6 most common mistakes in AI prompt writing are: being too vague with no context, role, or format specified; combining multiple tasks in one prompt which splits the model's focus; not defining the audience so the AI defaults to generic, unfocused output; skipping format instructions so the AI chooses its own inconsistent structure; not iterating — one prompt rarely produces the final result and refinement is essential; and not assigning a role which causes the model to default to a generalist tone that is often too broad for the task."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What are the 6 steps to write an effective AI prompt?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The 6 steps to write an effective AI prompt are: Step 1 — Assign a role by telling the AI who it is, for example 'You are a freelance copywriter specializing in SaaS email campaigns.' Step 2 — State the task clearly with one instruction per prompt, for example 'Write a subject line for a re-engagement email.' Step 3 — Define the audience by specifying age, profession, or knowledge level, for example 'Startup founders who have not logged in for 30 days.' Step 4 — Set tone and format, for example 'Use a conversational tone. Output in 3 bullet points, each under 25 words.' Step 5 — Add constraints specifying word count, keywords to include, or things to avoid, for example 'Keep it under 150 words. Do not use exclamation marks.' Step 6 — Give an example of the style you want, which closes the gap between expectation and output faster than any other technique."
