        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does generative AI understand language the way humans do?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "No. LLMs process statistical relationships between tokens, not semantic meaning. GPT-4 and similar models predict the most probable next token based on training data patterns — not by reasoning or understanding in the human sense. This is why prompt specificity directly affects output accuracy — vague context produces vague, high-entropy predictions. Structured prompting constrains the probability distribution toward the intended answer, which is why prompt engineering techniques produce measurably better outputs than unstructured requests."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the difference between generative AI and a search engine?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A search engine retrieves indexed pages matching keyword patterns from existing content on the internet. Generative AI produces entirely new text by predicting token sequences based on statistical patterns learned during training. Search engines return existing content; generative AI generates novel content that did not previously exist. Neither approach guarantees factual accuracy without verification — which is why hallucination detection is a required skill in any professional AI content workflow."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Do prompt engineering skills require technical or coding knowledge?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "No. Structured prompting and effective AI prompts are based on clear communication of role, task, format, and constraints — not code. LinkedIn Learning's 2024 AI skills report lists prompt engineering as a top skill gap among non-technical professionals including writers, marketers, and analysts. The five core steps to improve prompt output — specifying context, setting temperature, applying role-task-format-constraint structure, iterating in-session, and version-controlling templates — require no programming knowledge whatsoever."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What causes AI hallucination and how can it be reduced?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Hallucination is caused by the probabilistic nature of token prediction — the model generates plausible-sounding text even when factual grounding is absent in its training data. A 2023 study by Johns Hopkins University found GPT-3.5 hallucinated on 19.5% of factual medical queries. Vectara's 2024 analysis found hallucination rates ranging from 3% (Claude 2) to 9.6% (Llama 2-13B) on a standardized summarization benchmark. The three primary mitigation strategies are: structured prompting with explicit constraints to narrow the model's probability space, low temperature settings of 0 to 0.3 for factual tasks, and external fact verification of all LLM-generated claims before professional use."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the context window and why does it affect output quality?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The context window is the maximum number of tokens an LLM can process in a single inference session. GPT-4 supports 128,000 tokens — approximately 96,000 words. Claude Sonnet 4.6 and Opus 4.6 support up to 1,000,000 tokens as of 2026 (Anthropic, 2026). Inputs exceeding the context window are truncated, causing the model to lose earlier instructions or content entirely — which degrades output quality. Research from Stanford (Liu et al., 2023) documents the 'lost in the middle' effect, where LLMs weight tokens at the start and end of context more heavily than those in the middle. Best practice is to place the most critical instructions at the beginning and end of long prompts."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is generative AI and how does it work technically?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Generative AI is a category of artificial intelligence that produces new content — text, images, code, and audio — by learning statistical patterns from large training datasets and predicting outputs based on input context. It uses a neural network architecture called a transformer, introduced in the 2017 Google Research paper 'Attention Is All You Need' by Vaswani et al. GPT-4 was trained on text exceeding 1 trillion tokens (OpenAI, 2023). Unlike retrieval-based systems, generative AI does not look up stored answers — it predicts the most probable next token given prior context. Model weights number in the hundreds of billions for frontier models and encode all learned statistical patterns."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What are the 7 key technical parameters that affect generative AI output quality?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The 7 key technical parameters that affect generative AI output quality are: 1) Training Data — the breadth and quality of data the model was trained on determines domain coverage and factual accuracy; 2) Tokens — LLMs process text in chunks of 3 to 4 characters each, and context window limits define how much text can be processed at once; 3) Temperature — controls output randomness from 0 (most deterministic) to 2 (most creative); 4) Prompt Engineering Techniques — structured input design that reduces post-editing requirements by up to 40% (McKinsey, 2023); 5) Structured Prompting — explicitly specifying role, task, format, and constraints; 6) Hallucination — a structural property of probabilistic text generation affecting all frontier LLMs at rates of 3 to 20% depending on task domain; and 7) Fine-Tuning vs Prompting — fine-tuning retrains the model on new data requiring significant compute resources, while prompting directs the existing model at inference time using text alone and requires no technical background."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What are the 5 steps to apply generative AI knowledge for better prompt engineering?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The 5 steps to apply generative AI knowledge for better prompt engineering are: Step 1 — Specify context window usage by placing the most important instructions at the start and end of the prompt, exploiting the 'lost in the middle' effect documented by Stanford (Liu et al., 2023). Step 2 — Set temperature explicitly: use 0 to 0.3 for factual, legal, or structured outputs and 0.7 to 1.0 for ideation and creative drafts — unspecified temperature defaults vary by platform and produce inconsistent outputs. Step 3 — Apply role-task-format-constraint structure to reduce output revision time by up to 40% (McKinsey, 2023). Step 4 — Iterate in-session with correction prompts rather than restarting — LLMs retain context within a session and correction prompts are more efficient. Step 5 — Version-control prompt templates by storing tested prompts with version notes, input variables, and expected output format to enable reuse across projects and team AI content workflows."
